sections.s2_technical:
Sharding is horizontal because it splits rows, not columns; each shard is a full schema holding a slice of the rows. This lets you scale writes and storage linearly by adding shards, which a single primary database cannot do. It differs from read replicas, which copy the whole dataset to scale reads only.

The shard key is the single most important decision. A good key spreads data and load evenly and keeps the common queries on a single shard. For a bank, account id or customer id is usually a strong key because most operations are scoped to one account. Common strategies are range-based, mapping key ranges to shards, which allows range scans but risks hotspots; and hash-based, hashing the key to a shard, which spreads load evenly but loses range locality.

```java
// Hash-based sharding: route a row to a shard by its key
public int shardFor(String accountId, int shardCount) {
    return Math.abs(accountId.hashCode()) % shardCount;
}
```

Cross-shard queries are the main pain. A query that must touch many shards, like "sum balances across all customers," has to scatter to every shard and gather the partial results, which is slow and complex. Cross-shard transactions are worse, requiring distributed-transaction protocols. The design goal is therefore to choose the shard key so that the vast majority of operations stay on one shard, and to denormalize or maintain secondary indexes for the few queries that would otherwise span shards.

```java
// Scatter-gather for an unavoidable cross-shard aggregate
public BigDecimal totalBalance(List<Shard> shards) {
    return shards.stream()
        .map(Shard::sumBalances)          // scatter: ask each shard
        .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);  // gather: combine partials
}
```

Resharding is the hardest operational challenge. Adding a shard with naive modulo hashing remaps almost all rows, so the standard mitigation is consistent hashing, which moves only a small fraction, or virtual shards, where many logical shards are pre-created and simply reassigned to new physical nodes without moving the hashing boundaries. A senior nuance is to plan sharding before you need it, because retrofitting a shard key onto a live system is extremely risky, and to avoid shard keys that create hotspots, such as sharding by a monotonically increasing timestamp which sends all new writes to one shard.

